The test only runs `enforce_eager=True` at a single 32-row shape. The production path (`VLLM_USE_BREAKABLE_CUDAGRAPH=1`) relies on capture/replay-specific mechanics that this never exercises:

- the identity handoffs `x is state["y"]` / `x is state["part"]` at capture time;
- `mqa_forward` recognizing its own calls at replay via `q.data_ptr() == run.s["q"].data_ptr()`;
- the Engram deferred all-gathers that must be flushed by layer 0's eager break (otherwise `RuntimeError` under capture).

It also doesn't cover:

- the M boundaries (17, 64/128 GEMV mode switches, `attn_spec` at M <= 128, 256);
- the mixed-step fallback (`q_rr` + `from_attn_out`);
- spec-decode rows.

Could the test add a cudagraph run (drop `enforce_eager`, capture sizes including e.g. 17, 128 and 256) and a mixed prefill+decode step? Graph-replay bugs here would silently corrupt outputs rather than crash.

Should this test follow the monokernel CI layout proposed in #63486 (https://github.com/vllm-project/vllm/pull/63486)? That PR:

- moves the DSv4.1 mono tests to `tests/models/deepseek_v41/amd_specialized_decode/` and the steps into a `monokernels.yaml` test area;
- adds a shared float64 reference (`mono_reference.py`) and per-stage I/O tests with tolerances derived from the formats involved;
- adds gsm8k nightly configs with a check that the monokernel ran on every rank.

This PR's test compares with vLLM's path at 12% / 25%-flip bounds, while the description says the kernels were checked against fp64 references during development. Could those fp64 stage checks be contributed on top of the shared reference instead of an end-to-end diff only? It would also avoid a third DSv4.1 test file in `tests/models/` that #63486 then has to move.
